Overall: Good concept, incomplete engineering specification.

I particularly want to emphasize three things:
1. Don't sacrifice simplicity for unnecessary features.
2. Don't sacrifice genuine security for impressive-sounding encryption terminology.
3. Don't promise universal compatibility when browser vendors control their own extension environments.
One final point: I can help design the architecture, write the source code, implement the extension, build the backend, develop the UI, create tests and prepare publishing documentation. You can retain ownership and control, subject to the relevant IP assignments and third-party licenses. However, no developer or AI can honestly guarantee zero vulnerabilities or universal browser compatibility.
What I would do next
I think the most valuable next step is to create a complete AegisMark 2.0 Master Development Specification.

It should replace the existing plan, not merely supplement it. I would include:
- Final feature list (MVP versus future releases).
- Complete system architecture.
- Encryption and key-management specification.
- Database schema and synchronization protocol.
- Threat model and security requirements.
- Browser-by-browser compatibility matrix.
- Frontend and backend technology decisions.
- UI/UX structure and design requirements.
- Free/Pro subscription and account architecture.
- Browser-store compliance checklists.
- Ownership and licensing requirements.
- Development milestones and acceptance tests.
That way, whoever builds AegisMark has a precise engineering blueprint instead of a collection of ambitious promises.